What differs between an Ignition artifact and its Quark oracle, and what does not
2026-09-09. Desktop 2 (Phoenix, Ryzen 7 8700G), resnet_env17, onnx only. NO NPU SESSION was
built and no hardware context was taken; this reads two files.

Command (from the repo root):
  python tools/quant_pair_diff.py \
      models/resnet50_ignition_cle_c64.onnx models/resnet50_quark_cle_c64.onnx \
      --label-a ignition --label-b quark

WHY: docs/BENCHMARKS.md's Ignition sections report parity against a fresh Quark oracle as
INT8_EXACT, which is the gate in quant/verify.py: zero structural node delta, zero byte
mismatch on any scale, zero-point or non-int8 initializer, and at most 1 LSB on the int8
weights, in practice 0. That is a claim about the NUMBERS. It is not a claim that the two
files are byte-identical, and they are not. This separates the two so the next question can
be asked precisely: the VitisAI EP compiles the ONNX file into a DPU program, and whether two
files that agree on every number but disagree on names and metadata compile to the SAME
program is something no parity gate here can answer.

file bytes
  ignition   resnet50_ignition_cle_c64.onnx             25661876 B  sha256:74f2b9a180e05b22
  quark      resnet50_quark_cle_c64.onnx                25670047 B  sha256:2df63ef320dcdb49

model metadata
  ignition   producer='Ignition' version='0.1.0a1' ir=8 opset=[17]
  quark      producer='quark.onnx' version='0.11rc1' ir=8 opset=[17, 1, 5, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1]

graph shape
  ignition   nodes=380 initializers=470 inputs=1 outputs=1
  quark      nodes=380 initializers=470 inputs=1 outputs=1

graph_diff -- the same gate scripts/quant-validate.sh applies
  structural node delta      : 0
  initializers byte-mismatch : 0
  int8 initializers compared : 108
  int8 weights over 0 LSB    : 0
  ok(0 LSB)                  : True
  ok(1 LSB)                  : True

names, which the numbers do not depend on
  node names identical in order : 209/380
  initializer name sets equal   : True

byte-size difference: 8171 B

READING.

Identical: 380 nodes, 470 initializers, one input, one output; zero structural node delta;
zero initializers differing by a byte; all 108 int8 initializers equal at 0 LSB, not 1; and
the initializer name sets are equal. Every number and every edge in the two graphs matches.

Different: 8,171 bytes, and all of it is non-numeric.
  - producer_name and producer_version, 'Ignition' 0.1.0a1 against 'quark.onnx' 0.11rc1.
  - the opset_import list. Ignition declares one entry, opset 17. Quark declares nine, the
    same opset 17 plus eight further domain entries.
  - 171 of 380 node names, the rest matching in order.

So the difference between the two files is producer metadata, extra domain declarations, and
names. That is exactly the material a compiler is entitled to ignore, and exactly the material
it is entitled not to.

WHAT IS NOT ANSWERED, AND WHY IT WAS NOT RUN
The test this sets up is: compile both files through the VitisAI EP with a fresh cache and
compare the resulting compiled.*.xmodel and 4x4.xclbin. If they match, parity extends from
the file to the program the silicon actually runs, which is a stronger claim than this repo
currently makes anywhere. If they differ, the difference names what the vendor compiler keys
on beyond the numbers, and the eight extra opset domains are the first suspect.

That was NOT run. The host-load check reported HOST_LOAD_VERDICT PEER: another session was
part-way through `pipelines/bisenetv2/3_quantize.py` on the CPU. An EP compile is CPU-heavy,
the repo's own wrappers refuse to start on a contended machine without --allow-busy, and
starting one here risked corrupting a measurement in flight that this session does not own.
The NPU itself was idle -- `xrt-smi examine -r aie-partitions` reported no hardware contexts.
Re-run the comparison on a clear machine.
